x86/kvm: route host counter reads through the Harmony TSC oracle

The nested-host guest includes KVM, whose native RDTSC inputs bypass Harmony's
exit-count clock on stock host KVM. Read IA32_TSC through RDMSR instead: the
outer MSR filter routes it to Consonance's existing virtual-time handler.
The return value remains in the architectural TSC tick domain. Keep the
ordered accessor fenced, and replace every native host counter read in KVM,
including its PMU, LAPIC and nested-VMX paths.

Disable the optional VMX preemption timer by default. A hardware countdown
continues in real time even when its deadline was computed from a virtual
counter; the nested-host profile uses the guest Linux timer path instead.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried under the repository's
kernel-patch exception. The armed instruction scan must still qualify all
remaining counter reads before publishing the nested-host kernel.

--- a/arch/x86/kvm/x86.h
+++ b/arch/x86/kvm/x86.h
@@ -5,10 +5,27 @@
 #include <linux/kvm_host.h>
 #include <asm/fpu/xstate.h>
 #include <asm/mce.h>
+#include <asm/msr.h>
 #include <asm/pvclock.h>
 #include "kvm_cache_regs.h"
 #include "kvm_emulate.h"
 #include "cpuid.h"
+
+static __always_inline u64 kvm_host_tsc(void)
+{
+	return native_rdmsrq(MSR_IA32_TSC);
+}
+
+static __always_inline u64 kvm_host_tsc_ordered(void)
+{
+	u64 value;
+
+	mb();
+	rmb();
+	value = kvm_host_tsc();
+	barrier();
+	return value;
+}
 
